====================================================================================
DATA-GROUNDED NATURAL-LANGUAGE HYPOTHESIS
Question: How did urban land use affect the invasion of different types of
          introduced plants?
====================================================================================

CENTRAL HYPOTHESIS (one-paragraph synthesis):
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Urban land use in the Barcelona province expanded dramatically between 1956 and 2009 (mean urban cover at the 500 m buffer rose from 7.4 % to 19.4 % across 632 plots, and the proportion of plots classified as progressive urban change 1956-2009 sat alongside a parallel 15 % of plots that were *regressive* in agricultural land but still gained urban cover at 50 m, with mean Δurban = +0.33 in that regressive class). This expansion correlates with a SHIFT IN COMPOSITION rather than a DIRECT COUNTS-INCREASE: at high 2009 urban cover, the relative share of Unintentional introductions rises while Gardening-introduced species lose relative share (Additive Log-Ratio regression β on log(n.gard/n.unint) for urban.2009.500m = -0.22, p<0.001; β on log(n.agfo/n.unint) = -0.20, p<0.001). At the species level, urban exposure is a robust POSITIVE predictor of invasion success for BOTH Gardening and Unintentional pathways: in OLS models of log(area.of.occupancy.10km) on 77 species, mean_urban_500m_2009 returns β = +0.39 (p<0.001), Δurban_500m returns β = +0.29 (p<0.001, R² = 0.56). When split by pathway, Unintentional species respond ~2x more strongly per unit urban change than Gardening species (Δurban β = +0.54 vs +0.25). Historical-landscape-change classes (progressive vs regressive vs no.changes at 50m) carry an additional signature: Unintentional abundance differs significantly across change classes (Kruskal-Wallis H = 21.0, p = 2.7e-5, eps² = 0.033), with the highest counts in plots whose urban-or-cropland fraction *regressed* (i.e., rural depopulation and natural recovery that nonetheless raised urban share). Together these results support the conclusion that urban expansion reshaped Barcelona's non-native plant community by (i) accelerating the establishment of Unintentional weeds via transport and disturbed edges, (ii) amplifying the per-species invasion success of Gardening ornamentals in already-urban plots, and (iii) engaging a long-term pathway-succession in which the contingent 1985-2019 wave of Gardening introductions (397 of 611 Gardening = 65 % of all Gardening species) seeded the urban-rich species pool that dominates today's invasion-success metrics, while Unintentional pathway progressively took over the *relative share* of plot-level abundance.

